  "authorizationNodeContract": {
    "id": "A1",
    "task": "TASK-260728-q5kjta",
    "statement": "The human authorization node names the matrix revision it authorizes, and that name must be the CURRENT revision of this contract. A revision that was reviewed and rejected is not an authorization for the revision that replaced it, even when the two happen to enumerate the same mutations. Rule P1 pins the task that SPENDS the authorization; rule A1 pins the task that GRANTS it.",
    "history": "Reviewer verdict 06 F1. r5 corrected TASK-260728-q5kjta's mutation set and stamped its scope with 'revision 2026-07-28.r5'. r6 then superseded r5 without re-pointing that stamp, so the authorization node named by AC5 and by humanAuthorizationNode asked the operator to approve the revision verdict 05 had rejected. r6 had added a board gate, but aimed it only at TASK-260715-3jloqy - the unattended consumer - so the authorization edge itself was the one live consumer nothing checked.",
    "revisionPinRule": "The node's live contract fields must contain the rendered revisionPhrase for the current revision, and must contain NO superseded revision label. The superseded set is DERIVED from revisionLog rather than hand-listed, so it cannot fall behind a bump. Append-only history fields are excluded: notes is a progress log where a past revision label is a true historical statement, exactly as revisionLog is excluded from rule S1's count scan.",
    "revisionPhraseTemplate": "revision {revision}",
    "digestAlternativeRejected": "Verdict 06 offered a content digest plus revision as an alternative pin. Rejected, with reasoning: a digest changes on every edit to this JSON including edits that change nothing the operator is asked to approve, so it would force a board write per revision without pinning anything the revision label does not already pin, and a stale digest fails identically to a stale revision. The label plus a derived banned-revision set and a gate that fails closed is the same guarantee at lower maintenance cost. The gate, not the pin format, is what closes the defect.",
    "requiredAuthorizations": [
      "Create exactly four macOS App IDs: works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel.",
      "Enable the Network Extensions capability on those four and no other capability.",
      "Generate one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered as a device.",
      "Name the current matrix revision the sitting authorizes."
    ],
    "forbiddenAuthorizations": [
      "Any App Group record, including group.works.relux.tunnel.",
      "The App Groups capability on any App ID.",
      "Any Keychain access-group portal mutation; keychain sharing has no portal record.",
      "Any of the four iOS App IDs, which are deferred under ADR-024.",
      "Any Developer ID, App Store, or other distribution profile.",
      "Any change to the legacy works.relux.proxy identity."
    ],
    "boardGate": {
      "fields": [
        "description",
        "scope",
        "ac"
      ],
      "excludedFields": [
        "notes"
      ],
      "requiredPhrases": [
        "works.relux.tunnel.mac",
        "works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel",
        "works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac",
        "works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel",
        "Network Extensions",
        "Mac Development",
        "ADR-024"
      ],
      "requiredPhraseNote": "Each phrase is matched with a right boundary, so works.relux.tunnel.mac is NOT satisfied by the occurrence inside works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel. The four App IDs are additionally required as a DERIVED set from c1AuthorizationScope, so this hand-written list cannot silently authorize a different set.",
      "negationScope": "clause",
      "clauseBoundaries": "[.;:]\\s+  or  ,\\s+(and|but|nor)\\s+",
      "negationMarkers": [
        "no",
        "not",
        "never",
        "without",
        "excluded",
        "deferred",
        "nor"
      ],
      "negationNote": "Identifiers the ceremony must NOT touch do appear in the node's text - that is how an exclusion is stated - so the gate requires every CLAUSE that mentions one to carry a negation marker, rather than requiring absence. Attribution is per clause, not per character window: AC4 states its exclusion AFTER the identifiers it excludes, so a preceding-context check would miss the real text, while a window wide enough to reach backwards over a list of four App IDs is also wide enough to let a neighbouring clause's 'NOT' cover an identifier that has quietly become authorized. An r7 board negative gate demonstrated exactly that against a 220-character symmetric window, which is why the window was replaced by clause attribution. Clause splitting is safe despite these identifiers being full of dots, because a dot INSIDE an identifier is never followed by whitespace.",
      "negatedPhraseSource": "DERIVED: c1AuthorizationScope.explicitlyNotAuthorized identifiers, every appGroups[].portalRecordIdentifier, and legacy.bundleIdentifier."
    },
    "gate": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_check-portal-consumer.py checks BOTH live consumers named by this contract - the authorization node (rule A1) and the portal mutation task (rule P1) - and exits non-zero on drift in either. validate_matrix.py cannot read the board; R34 only checks that this pin is complete and agrees with c1AuthorizationScope and with the revision bookkeeping.",
    "source": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-06.md finding F1; c1AuthorizationScope and revisionLog in this contract; ADR-028."
  },
  "humanAuthorizationNode": "TASK-260728-q5kjta",
  "authorizesPortalMutationBy": "TASK-260715-3jloqy",
  "portalMutationTaskContract": {
    "id": "P1",
    "task": "TASK-260715-3jloqy",
    "statement": "The unattended portal mutation task consumes the human authorization granted at Ceremony C1, so its board contract must state the SAME mutation set as c1AuthorizationScope. Correcting the authorization node alone does not correct the task that spends the authorization.",
    "history": "Reviewer verdict 05 F2. r5 corrected TASK-260728-q5kjta - the human ceremony - to the post-G4/K2 scope, and left TASK-260715-3jloqy, the task that actually performs the mutations, carrying the pre-r5 contract: create 'shared groups', provision 'packet-tunnel, App Group, and Keychain capabilities', AC1 requiring that 'shared groups exist', and AC2 requiring the packet-tunnel entitlement 'only on provider identifiers'. The last of those is a material defect independent of App Groups: Apple requires com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, so a provider-only rule would under-provision both macOS host App IDs and fail the build this matrix exists to make possible.",
    "requiredMutations": [
      "Create exactly four macOS App IDs: works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel.",
      "Enable the Network Extensions capability on all four, hosts included.",
      "Generate one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered.",
      "Inspect every downloaded profile and assert the unsuffixed packet-tunnel-provider value on HOST and PROVIDER profiles alike."
    ],
    "forbiddenMutations": [
      "Any App Group record, including group.works.relux.tunnel, which is allocated at the later iOS sitting under ADR-024, and including the deleted probe record.",
      "The App Groups capability on any App ID.",
      "Any Keychain access-group portal mutation; keychain sharing has no portal record at all.",
      "Any of the four iOS App IDs.",
      "Any Developer ID, App Store, or other distribution profile.",
      "Any change to the legacy works.relux.proxy identity."
    ],
    "requiredNegativeAssertions": [
      "A16 - no macOS bundle and no Mac Development profile Entitlements dict declares com.apple.security.application-groups.",
      "A17 - no macOS bundle declares keychain-access-groups. Asserted against the BUNDLE, not the profile: a Mac Development profile legitimately carries the team-wide keychain-access-groups = ['<TEAMID>.*'] wildcard and that wildcard is not a finding."
    ],
    "bannedContractPhrases": [
      "shared groups",
      "App Group, and Keychain capabilities",
      "only on provider identifiers"
    ],
    "bannedPhraseNote": "These three phrases are the exact pre-r5 wording. They are listed so that the board-contract gate can assert their ABSENCE mechanically, and so that a future editor who reintroduces one is contradicting a named rule rather than an unwritten intent.",
    "boardGate": {
      "fields": [
        "description",
        "scope",
        "ac"
      ],
      "requiredPhrases": [
        "works.relux.tunnel.mac",
        "works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel",
        "works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac",
        "works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel",
        "Network Extensions",
        "hosts included",
        "packet-tunnel-provider",
        "ADR-024"
      ],
      "requiredPhraseNote": "Each phrase is matched with a right boundary, so works.relux.tunnel.mac is NOT satisfied by the occurrence inside works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel. 'hosts included' is the phrase that has to survive: it is the direct negation of the removed AC2 clause 'only on provider identifiers', and it is the one whose loss would silently under-provision both host App IDs."
    },
    "gate": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_check-portal-consumer.py reads this object and the live board record for the task and exits non-zero on any drift. It is the durable form of the ad-hoc consistency check reviewer verdict 05 F2 ran by hand. Since r7 the same script also checks the authorization node under rule A1, so both live consumers this contract names are gated by one harness. It is a BOARD gate, not a contract gate: validate_matrix.py cannot read the board, and only checks that this pin is complete and agrees with c1AuthorizationScope.",
    "source": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-05.md finding F2; c1AuthorizationScope in this contract; Apple, Configuring Network Extension capability - the containing app and the embedded provider each carry the entitlement."
  },
  "networkExtensionRule": {
    "id": "NE1",
    "statement": "The com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension entitlement is carried by BOTH the containing app and the embedded provider. Its value depends on the SIGNING CHANNEL, not on the role and not on the packaging alone: direct Developer ID signing uses the values with the '-systemextension' suffix; every other channel — App Store distribution AND development signing — uses the values without that suffix.",
    "source": "Apple DTS, https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/800887 — 'When you build an app with an embedded NE extension, both the app and the extension must be signed with the com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension entitlement. This is a restricted entitlement, that is, it must be authorised by a provisioning profile.' and 'The value of this entitlement is an array, and the values in that array differ depend on your distribution channel: If you distribute your app directly with Developer ID signing, use the values with the -systemextension suffix. Otherwise - including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development - use the values without that suffix.'",
    "profileWarning": "Apple DTS, same source: 'Make sure you authorise these values with your provisioning profile. If, for example, you use an App Store distribution profile with a Developer ID signed app, things won't work because the profile doesn't authorise the right values.' The profile class and the entitlement value must therefore agree, which is why every value in this contract is keyed by channel.",
    "baseValue": "packet-tunnel-provider",
    "suffix": "-systemextension",
    "restrictedEntitlement": true,
    "empiricalEvidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac with `codesign -d --entitlements :-`. Three shipping Developer-ID-distributed packet-tunnel products carry the suffixed value on BOTH the containing app and the embedded system extension: Tailscale (io.tailscale.ipn.macsys and .network-extension), PureVPN (com.purevpn.app.mac with .wireguard-packet-tunnel and com.purevpn.mac.packet-tunnel), Surfshark (com.surfshark.vpnclient.macos.direct and .PacketTunnel-WireGuard). No development-signed counterpart was available locally, so the development-channel value rests on the DTS statement above and is re-verified by assertion A3 at first signed build."
  },
  "keychainScopeRule": {
    "id": "K1",
    "statement": "On macOS the file-based (system-domain) keychain is controlled by per-item SecAccess ACLs. keychain-access-groups applies only to the Data Protection Keychain, which is unavailable outside a user context. A root-context provider targeting the system-domain keychain therefore never needs this entitlement, and the presence of the key in a shipping third-party provider is not evidence that it functions.",
    "replaces": "The amendmentRule formerly carried on targets[macos.provider].entitlements[keychain-access-groups]. That rule said the row would be amended to required if the resolved transport gave the provider direct keychain access. Its antecedent is now TRUE - candidate D does give the provider direct keychain access - while its consequent is FALSE, because the file-based keychain has no access groups. Left in place it was a live instruction to re-grant the row on exactly the reasoning TASK-260728-7ii1xz refutes.",
    "reopeningCondition": "The macos.provider row reopens only if the provider is moved into a user context, not merely because it touches a keychain.",
    "shippingProductsAreNotEvidence": "PureVPN's packet-tunnel system extensions carry keychain-access-groups. Under this rule that observation may not be used to argue the row back open: a key present in a signed bundle is not proof that the mechanism it names is the one the process uses.",
    "source": "Apple TN3137 'On Mac keychain APIs and implementations'; TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md sections 4-5 and 7.",
    "hostScope": "K1 governs the macOS PROVIDER only. It says nothing about the macOS host, which does run in a user context and does use the Data Protection Keychain, and that silence is what let r4 keep a shared access group on macos.host with no second member to share with. Rule K2 closes it: sharing is the entitlement's only function, so a host with no co-member is served by its default access group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1.",
    "osFloorScope": "K1's 'never needs this entitlement' clause is unconditional and stays that way - the Data Protection Keychain has no meaning in a root context on any OS version. What K1 did NOT state is that its supporting clause, 'the sandbox already grants what it needs', was read on macOS 26.5 alone. Rule K3 owns that version scope: it names the macOS 15.0 floor, names the owner that closes it, and pre-authorizes the temporary FILE exception an absent floor grant would require. That exception is not this entitlement, so arming it never reopens this row. Reviewer verdict 08 F1."
  },
  "keychainLeastPrivilegeRule": {
    "id": "K2",
    "statement": "keychain-access-groups is granted only to a target that must share a Data Protection Keychain item with a NAMED second target in this contract. A target that only reads and writes its own items is already served by the default access group the signing toolchain gives it, so the entitlement would add signed authority with no consumer. On iOS the host and the appex are two such members and both are granted. On macOS there is no second member today, so the key is prohibited on all four macOS targets.",
    "defaultAccessGroupLaw": "The system forms each app's access-group array as the keychain-access-groups entitlement array first, then the application identifier, then the app groups; the FIRST element is the default group that keychain services uses when a caller names none. Two consequences this contract depends on. (a) A target with no keychain-access-groups entitlement still has a private default group equal to <TeamID>.<bundle id>, so withholding never costs a target access to its own items. (b) Withholding MOVES the default: macos.host's default group changes from 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared under r4 to 262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac under r5. A consumer must not paper that over with an explicit kSecAttrAccessGroup naming the shared group - on macOS the correct call names no access group at all, and naming the shared one returns errSecMissingEntitlement.",
    "sharingIsTheOnlyFunction": "The entitlement confers exactly one thing beyond the default group: membership of a group that another target can also join. It does not unlock the Data Protection Keychain, does not change item durability, and does not survive anything the default group does not. Any argument for granting it that does not name a second reading target is therefore an argument for something the entitlement does not do.",
    "relationToK1": "K1 explains why the macOS PROVIDER can never be a member: the file-based system-domain keychain it uses has no access groups. K2 is the rule K1 did not cover - why the macOS HOST, which does use the Data Protection Keychain, still gets no custom group. Reviewer verdict 04 F1 named exactly that gap: the provider-side test was applied and the host-side one was not.",
    "reopeningCondition": "Per-row, in targets[].entitlements['keychain-access-groups'].reopensOnly. A row reopens only on a named second member, never on convenience or cross-platform symmetry.",
    "source": "Apple, Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/sharing-access-to-keychain-items-among-a-collection-of-apps); Apple TN3137, On Mac keychain APIs and implementations (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3137-on-mac-keychains). Both fetched 2026-07-28.",
    "settledByDecisionNotOmission": "Every withheld keychain row states its own reopening condition, on both platforms and in both families. r1-r4 left the four probe rows prohibited with no such condition, which made them settled by omission - indistinguishable from an oversight, and reopenable by anyone who read the silence as 'nobody considered it'. Rule R31 now rejects a withheld row that states no reopening condition, which is the same discipline rule G4 already applies to App Groups."
  },
  "keychainSandboxFloorRule": {
    "id": "K3",
    "statement": "The conclusion that the macOS provider needs no keychain-related entitlement rests on a BASE OS SANDBOX GRANT, which is an OS-version-scoped fact about the running system rather than an invariant of this contract. It holds on the OS versions where it has actually been read. On any supported version below that it is UNVERIFIED, and this contract models it as unresolved instead of settling it by silence.",
    "verifiedOn": "macOS 26.5 (25F71). /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb lines 792-793 grant file-read* and file-write* on the /Library/Keychains subpath to any process holding com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension, and lines 655-656 allow the com.apple.securityd.xpc and com.apple.SecurityServer Mach lookups unconditionally. Re-read and confirmed unchanged for r10.",
    "unverifiedAtFloor": "macOS 15.0 - the binding deployment floor for generated targets, set by .spec/platform-distribution.md 'Minimum platform policy' and selected exactly in .research/260720_task-260715-3r0993-project-generator-deployment-target-policy.md, which also names macos-15 as the minimum hosted CI lane. No macOS 15 host, virtual machine, installer, or SDK-resident sandbox profile exists in this task's environment - only Xcode 26.5 and macOS 26 SDKs - so the floor profile could not be read here. Apple does not document application.sb contents, so no secondary source closes it either. This is a stated gap, not an inference.",
    "whyItMatters": "This matrix is the source for generated entitlements and for the automated allowlist checks. If the floor's base profile does NOT carry the grant, the provider needs a temporary FILE EXCEPTION to reach the System-keychain transport TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected. With no model for that case, the required exception would be rejected as an unreviewed relaxation under rule X1 and assertion A10a, on a supported OS - a silent rejection of a real requirement. Reviewer verdict 08 F1.",
    "resolutionOwner": "TASK-260715-1uxx3i - it already owns the credential-free Apple build matrix whose minimum-macOS lane runs on the macos-15 hosted runner, and reading /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb there is a credential-free static read squarely inside its AC2 static checks. No board element is created for this: the floor lane already exists, and it already reaches this contract transitively through TASK-260715-uyju7n on the live board.",
    "runtimeConsumer": "TASK-260715-1o9wjz - the provider-context resolver whose macOS path actually opens the System keychain. Its provider-context tests are where an absent grant surfaces as a runtime failure rather than as a build finding, so it is the second place the answer lands.",
    "ifGrantAbsentAtFloor": "Arm register entry X1-C1: com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write valued /Library/Keychains/ on macos.provider only, on its two macOS channels. Arming is PRE-AUTHORIZED by this revision, so the exception is a reviewed one the moment the evidence exists and is never an unreviewed relaxation under rule X1 - the review is recorded here, the evidence belongs to the owner. It is an amendment under section 11 that moves the row status and the register entry and changes nothing else.",
    "ifGrantPresentAtFloor": "Record the floor evidence against this rule and retire entry X1-C1. The macos.provider keychain decision then needs no further change: the row stays prohibited under rule K1, on the reason K1 already gives.",
    "notAPortalCapability": "A temporary file exception is a signing-time App Sandbox entitlement. It is not an App ID capability, carries no portal record, and cannot be created or authorized in the Developer portal. Arming it therefore adds nothing to Ceremony C1, whose authorized entries stay exactly as c1AuthorizationScope states them.",
    "ceremonyC1Impact": "none",
    "productEvidenceIsNotProof": "Re-read on this Mac for r10. Surfshark's packet-tunnel system extension declares LSMinimumSystemVersion 10.15 and ships com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write covering /private/var/db/mds/, /Library/Keychains/ and a further private path; PureVPN's two providers declare 10.15 and carry keychain-access-groups; Tailscale's declares 12.4 and carries no keychain entitlement at all. A support range opening at 10.15 spans OS versions this project does not support, and a bundled defensive path set is not a targeted floor fix, so none of the three establishes either necessity or redundancy at macOS 15.0. Rule K1's clause runs in both directions here: a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.",
    "source": "Reviewer verdict 08 F1. /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71); .spec/platform-distribution.md minimum platform policy; Apple TN3137."
  },
  "appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule": {
    "id": "G4",
    "statement": "com.apple.security.application-groups is granted only to a target whose OWN currently selected design uses one of the grants the entitlement actually confers on that platform. A possible future transport, a spec sentence, the shape of a target it resembles, and the cost of a second portal sitting are none of them a function. On iOS the two production targets qualify. The iOS probe pair does not: its own contract names no shared-container function, and G4 tests the target's own design, never an inherited one. On macOS no target qualifies today, so the key is prohibited on every macOS target, production and probe alike.",
    "surveyBasis": "The entitlement's macOS effect is not a matter of opinion: it is two sandbox-array-entitlement blocks in the system App Sandbox profile. Both were read in full at /System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71) rather than inferred from documentation. They are the only two occurrences of the key in that profile.",
    "survey": [
      {
        "grant": "file-read-data on (application-group-literal suite \"\") and read-write-and-issue-extensions plus file-ioctl/mknod/revoke/search/mount/unmount on (application-group-regex suite \"/\")",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:305-315",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "The group container itself. The host resolves it under the logged-in user's home, the root provider under /private/var/root, so it is not a shared container on macOS. Directly corroborated: two shipping packet-tunnel system extensions on this Mac ship temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/, which is what you author when you need to cross that boundary."
      },
      {
        "grant": "network-bind and network-outbound on (application-group-regex suite \"/\")",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:307-314",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "UNIX-domain sockets inside the group container. Same home-relative defect. It is also redundant for private use: application.sb:754-756 already allows network-bind and network-outbound on the target's OWN container with no entitlement at all."
      },
      {
        "grant": "file-read* and process-exec on /Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:316-321",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "App Store-delivered group content. This project ships development and Developer ID on macOS and never the Mac App Store (.spec/platform-distribution.md), so the path is never populated."
      },
      {
        "grant": "mach-lookup and mach-register on (global-name-prefix (string-append suite \".\"))",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:326-328",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "The XPC channel, and the only grant that ever made the macOS App Group look load-bearing. TASK-260728-7ii1xz evaluated a custom XPC Mach service as candidate C and REJECTED it: it requires an exception entitlement that rule X1 puts under review, and it is dominated by the selected candidate D. With no XPC service there is no prefix to authorize."
      },
      {
        "grant": "ipc-posix* on (ipc-posix-name-prefix (string-append suite \"/\"))",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:329",
        "namespace": "system-wide",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": true,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "POSIX shared memory and semaphores in a group-prefixed namespace. This one does cross the root/user boundary and is easy to miss. No candidate transport proposed it, the accepted M1 lifecycle contract does not use it, and it is named here so that a future design cannot claim the entitlement was withheld without the option being seen."
      },
      {
        "grant": "read-only-and-issue-extensions and process-exec on ~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>",
        "sandboxProfileLines": "application.sb:459-465",
        "namespace": "home-relative",
        "worksAcrossRootAndUserContexts": false,
        "usedBySelectedDesign": false,
        "note": "Scripting support. Home-relative, and this project ships no user scripts."
      }
    ],
    "privateContainerFinding": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz amendment M6 left the rows granted on the grounds that 'a private root-context container may still have uses this task did not survey'. Surveyed here, and the answer is no. (appsandbox-container-common) and (appsandbox-container-macos) at application.sb:107-108 are UNCONDITIONAL - they are not inside any (when (entitlement ...)) form - so a sandboxed target already has its own private container, derived from the application_container_id sandbox parameter, without any App Group entitlement. The only thing a group container adds over the container the provider already has is SHARING, and sharing is precisely what the root/user split breaks. A private group container is therefore strictly redundant, not merely unused.",
    "selectedTransport": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected candidate D: the provider owns a file-based system-domain keychain item, seeded once from the containing app over NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage, with the non-secret profile snapshot carried in providerConfiguration. Keychain, NE app message, providerConfiguration - none of the three is an App Group mechanism.",
    "iOSUnaffected": "On iOS the host and the appex run as the same user in the same context, so the group container and UserDefaults suite are genuinely shared and the .spec/architecture.md:111 design holds unchanged for PRODUCTION. ios.host and ios.provider keep the entitlement as required.",
    "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
    "ceremonyCostIsZero": "Withholding introduces no new human ceremony, which removes the operational argument verdict 03 rejected. The four iOS App IDs are already in c1AuthorizationScope.explicitlyNotAuthorized under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning already requires a later portal sitting that C1 does not cover. The App Group record is allocated at that sitting, alongside the iOS App IDs that are its only consumers. Nothing that C1 could have granted today is needed today. (r6: this sentence counted r4's two records until rule S1's count scan caught it - the third instance of the stale-summary class reviewer verdict 05 F1 named, and the one neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted.)",
    "source": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71), read in full at lines 302-330 and 459-465. TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md sections 4, 5 and 6. Apple, Configuring app groups: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups",
    "probeRule": "r5, reviewer verdict 04 F3. A probe inherits identifiers and profile classes from the production shape it rehearses, but it does NOT inherit entitlements. Each probe row is tested against the probe task's own scope and acceptance criteria. TASK-260715-1jckn0 (iOS) and TASK-260715-1r0fxv (macOS) both scope their probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle plus a versioned app message, and neither names a shared container, so no probe target on either platform is granted an App Group. If a probe is meant to prove that a capability can be provisioned at all, that is a VERIFICATION deliverable and must be written into the probe task as an acceptance criterion first; the entitlement then follows the criterion, not the other way round."
  },
