id:TASK-260728-q5kjta
name:conduct-c1-apple-permission-ceremony
status:backlog
description:THE single up-front human permission sitting (Ceremony C1) on the current Apple-silicon Mac. This task exists so the board has exactly one human-input node for every up-front grant, instead of scattering grants across four tasks that a human would have to revisit across producer-reviewer cycles. The human performs all grants in one session; every downstream evidence task (TASK-260715-apc34w, TASK-260715-3jloqy, TASK-260728-dveo1o, TASK-260717-ziprhs) then runs unattended using the access granted here. The human is never asked to wait for a review cycle. No secret value is ever requested, echoed, logged, screenshotted, committed, or stored on the board.
scope:In scope, all in one sitting: unlocking the login Keychain and granting always-allow private-key access for the Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities to the signing tools that run unattended; authenticating Xcode and the Apple Developer portal for the Relux Works team including two-factor; authorizing creation and download of exactly the macOS entries in the approved identifier and entitlement matrix (revision 2026-07-28.r11) and nothing beyond them, namely the four macOS App IDs works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac and works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel, the Network Extensions capability on those four and no other capability, and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Apple-silicon Mac registered as a device; explicitly NOT creating any App Group record, NOT enabling the App Groups capability on any App ID, and NOT performing any Keychain access group mutation, because matrix rule G4 prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, rule K2 prohibits keychain-access-groups on every macOS target, and keychain sharing has no portal record at all; running notarytool store-credentials interactively to write a NAMED profile into the login Keychain; choosing and stating the owner disposition for the source App Store Connect API private-key file; generating the Sparkle ed25519 keypair with the pinned vendor tool and placing the private key into approved custody; recording only non-secret grant facts. Out of scope: approving any system VPN or system extension dialog (that is Approval A1 and later Hold H2, and no signed artifact exists yet); provisioning the deferred iOS identifiers; creating any Developer ID or App Store distribution profile; performing the portal mutations themselves (TASK-260715-3jloqy); validating profiles, verifying notary authentication, or recording public-key evidence (owned by the downstream evidence tasks); any product code; any owner product decision.
ac:1. Every up-front grant is performed in ONE human session and the session record states the start and end time and that no producer or reviewer cycle occurred inside it. 2. The login Keychain is unlocked and always-allow private-key access is granted for the Apple Development and Developer ID Application identities to the named unattended signing tools; the record names the identities by common name and the tools granted, never key material. 3. An authenticated Xcode/Apple Developer portal session for the Relux Works team exists and the operator has confirmed authority to create the macOS entries in the approved matrix; two-factor is completed during this sitting. 4. Creation and download of exactly the approved macOS entries is authorized and nothing beyond them: the four macOS App IDs works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac and works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel; the Network Extensions capability on those four; and one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Mac registered. The record states explicitly that NO App Group record was created, that the App Groups capability was enabled on no App ID, that no Keychain access group portal mutation was performed or required, and that no Developer ID or App Store distribution profile was created. All FOUR iOS App IDs (works.relux.tunnel.ios, works.relux.tunnel.ios.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.ios, works.relux.tunnel.probe.ios.tunnel) are explicitly NOT authorized because iOS is deferred under ADR-024, and works.relux.proxy is not touched. 5. A NAMED notarytool credential profile is stored in the login Keychain and the owner states the disposition for the source App Store Connect API private-key file; only the profile name and the chosen disposition are recorded, never the file path, key ID, issuer ID, passphrase, or key bytes. 6. The Sparkle ed25519 keypair is generated with the pinned vendor tool and the private key is placed in approved custody; only the custody location name is recorded here. 7. Shell history and command echo are suppressed for every credential command, and a post-ceremony scan of the repository, board resources, run logs, and shell history finds no secret value, key path, key ID, issuer ID, or account credential. 8. A TASK-ID-scoped outcome records which grants succeeded, which were declined, and any residual interaction the portal may still force later; a declined grant is recorded as a named blocker on the specific downstream task rather than as an assumed grant.
