    "summary": "Two least-privilege corrections and one board-metadata correction. F1: keychain-access-groups is now PROHIBITED on macos.host and therefore on all four macOS targets. New rule K2 states the test K1 did not cover - the entitlement's only function is sharing, and the macOS host has no second member to share with, because rule K1 excludes the root/system-domain provider and neither probe touches the Data Protection Keychain. The host keeps full access to its own items through the default access group the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides. K2 also records the ordering law that makes withholding safe and its one real consequence: the macOS default group moves from the shared literal to the app-ID literal, which TASK-260715-379cpk must be authored against. F3: com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider, because TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container; G4 gains an explicit probeRule saying a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes but never entitlements. That left group.works.relux.tunnel.probe with no consumer, so the record is DELETED and G3 is restated as the stronger property that no probe carries a group at all. One App Group record remains. F2: TASK-260728-q5kjta's scope and AC4 were corrected on the board to name the same four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions only, zero App Group records and capabilities, no Keychain portal mutation, the Mac Development profile class, and all four excluded iOS App IDs. Every r2/r3/r4 decision the reviewer listed as acceptable is preserved.",
